 product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }((2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(6\\pi +0+0)-(3\\pi +0+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97712/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97712"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection/513f0f2fb21c158a737cb49badb4f563fb5f8d2f8e5b774b25f94436f1db116b" :answer/id #uuid "b860d49b-45b1-56a0-b923-5dac24c33f3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection/948ddd1f85ff8cfead2e53ee61c5247d5a257573e741cf2397ec648d3a5007dc" :answer/id #uuid "b15945df-fbbb-5ef3-b4de-9e50b2425110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection/6d16f10bd35b8a40602543197c187b2bed7446cb10f03f52239e15bccc7a4183" :answer/id #uuid "9d9ebcc3-4857-583e-b281-074cb0eb6d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection/2759a6c0e69cef40d6206c0649f4684b1057b660b6fbadb3a6addb3587e90440" :answer/id #uuid "7de7c998-a09f-5750-a9b5-aa4dc8b1ccf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection/37b466975921ec1194bd84183a89ad528a21b7ca065d601276110532c2be9492" :answer/id #uuid "cb20cb48-9c1d-55d1-8a85-5b808463d433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b121e9c3-f2c7-5ee2-bf76-6405555aff01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97726/selection/513f0f2fb21c158a737cb49badb4f563fb5f8d2f8e5b774b25f94436f1db116b" :answer-field/choices ["q-97726/selection/513f0f2fb21c158a737cb49badb4f563fb5f8d2f8e5b774b25f94436f1db116b" "q-97726/selection/948ddd1f85ff8cfead2e53ee61c5247d5a257573e741cf2397ec648d3a5007dc" "q-97726/selection/6d16f10bd35b8a40602543197c187b2bed7446cb10f03f52239e15bccc7a4183" "q-97726/selection/2759a6c0e69cef40d6206c0649f4684b1057b660b6fbadb3a6addb3587e90440" "q-97726/selection/37b466975921ec1194bd84183a89ad528a21b7ca065d601276110532c2be9492"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97726" :question/id #uuid "5fae5da3-e57f-5671-91de-202da267e1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97726" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97726/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97726"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection/5b7ddf009b64ded4108192f12330ca0ac3c4e825edc5879a05d6d6a6b48f7658" :answer/id #uuid "46dbf34c-4240-5507-808e-b3b6c54cb1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection/418f6bd5cfe04942098fb1b248b8de88a65c6478be4ffc53c082410287454bb2" :answer/id #uuid "c5a0765c-c75c-53a4-8107-43ded4173630" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection/118c2e46c2f277aac3c397cbfa3d47d859be027e1914faa81f31a2b572d026c0" :answer/id #uuid "4e830c27-e5cb-5916-8f67-c7aef0e53df8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection/26a03cafba73b532e2cdc68d6e3188995be066a2e2769e9d6c0f8a8a8fc963a0" :answer/id #uuid "7a4b9b81-5a16-5bb5-9d00-e657245d3072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection/9535d08a357906505cbd729c67cbf8d57236f3aef4d2a086304e7c44d1669be5" :answer/id #uuid "a38e81e7-84e3-53b7-925e-3eb09ea6ad9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6387a3c0-8fd8-5255-a956-ebb777c4aa46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97725/selection/118c2e46c2f277aac3c397cbfa3d47d859be027e1914faa81f31a2b572d026c0" :answer-field/choices ["q-97725/selection/5b7ddf009b64ded4108192f12330ca0ac3c4e825edc5879a05d6d6a6b48f7658" "q-97725/selection/418f6bd5cfe04942098fb1b248b8de88a65c6478be4ffc53c082410287454bb2" "q-97725/selection/118c2e46c2f277aac3c397cbfa3d47d859be027e1914faa81f31a2b572d026c0" "q-97725/selection/26a03cafba73b532e2cdc68d6e3188995be066a2e2769e9d6c0f8a8a8fc963a0" "q-97725/selection/9535d08a357906505cbd729c67cbf8d57236f3aef4d2a086304e7c44d1669be5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97725" :question/id #uuid "d095eb6a-2ae3-5699-bca7-a936ad3d6b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97725" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection/8007ac72d5284742d366a3d69c437b11c60ab580b4f2bc8b6b25f517e815c71e" :answer/id #uuid "49cdd3a2-06c8-54df-9499-74e4574715a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection/686ae5c944bbe3848ae8842af6ff519c6a8f838707160c48dfe397c3d00594aa" :answer/id #uuid "e7b0ecd9-1e11-514e-9d00-dabbaa932d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection/c3956d78bfadc6a91ce69116a893f03c5042a90d50fb36d74633cd2dfbf9cee6" :answer/id #uuid "57bc95a7-7876-5edd-918f-60b825ca10c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{t}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection/5b15a8e9d290f3b825ba2a11e75a8dd35737fff04cf3be40a11d2ba07ed95bfd" :answer/id #uuid "092efdca-3c93-5998-ac53-82de3cf614cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection/1ca8ef21327293d8498efdc17ff3c2b2e24178ee87d3015351805304ed7d3954" :answer/id #uuid "9ca2cfff-9996-5647-81bf-56d33d2fd79d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5cbcd1-b3e0-5621-bd4b-2aa9e3877d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-43926/selection/686ae5c944bbe3848ae8842af6ff519c6a8f838707160c48dfe397c3d00594aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-43926/selection/8007ac72d5284742d366a3d69c437b11c60ab580b4f2bc8b6b25f517e815c71e" "q-43926/selection/686ae5c944bbe3848ae8842af6ff519c6a8f838707160c48dfe397c3d00594aa" "q-43926/selection/c3956d78bfadc6a91ce69116a893f03c5042a90d50fb36d74633cd2dfbf9cee6" "q-43926/selection/5b15a8e9d290f3b825ba2a11e75a8dd35737fff04cf3be40a11d2ba07ed95bfd" "q-43926/selection/1ca8ef21327293d8498efdc17ff3c2b2e24178ee87d3015351805304ed7d3954"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-43926" :question/id #uuid "d5cdc838-4f6b-5bb7-a174-36b0b232f02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43926" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=1-\\sqrt{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(1-2\\sqrt{t}+t),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t-\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\sqrt{t})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-43926/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-43926"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-42741"] :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection/88c07ab1979b28bbdacd16f4d20514ba25d6b72f00d9771342e36cfc3e1ac993" :answer/id #uuid "2a79ae09-2af0-5a45-b73f-df971c273f63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2-3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection/18b43156905a11eedc356de141c9914d5f8ae2e23dadb4f3014b7696d62bb71d" :answer/id #uuid "a42e1b67-0009-55bc-8150-c3e441a30aa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(2-3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection/2b07b0119eb4529c615a81f72af566a7aafbcd195dc6938ad6fe001771672278" :answer/id #uuid "3e432c84-b564-5ea2-9ef1-8ccfe3209b99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-3t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection/86d21c85af02fd74c3db02a0fcecd44ef9340f7569464714cd554a6f08a45377" :answer/id #uuid "8aa81a03-683d-5b3c-919b-d9e0d3beb28f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2-3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection/6b87e413aa104714be4edaf077777a42a25ef41b3fe6952ba7e14744d565fe1f" :answer/id #uuid "366aae35-1927-5ea2-b1da-cc1e8e766e38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(3t-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c90574b-107a-5d1f-ace1-e8e7f2db2ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97721/selection/2b07b0119eb4529c615a81f72af566a7aafbcd195dc6938ad6fe001771672278" :answer-field/choices ["q-97721/selection/88c07ab1979b28bbdacd16f4d20514ba25d6b72f00d9771342e36cfc3e1ac993" "q-97721/selection/18b43156905a11eedc356de141c9914d5f8ae2e23dadb4f3014b7696d62bb71d" "q-97721/selection/2b07b0119eb4529c615a81f72af566a7aafbcd195dc6938ad6fe001771672278" "q-97721/selection/86d21c85af02fd74c3db02a0fcecd44ef9340f7569464714cd554a6f08a45377" "q-97721/selection/6b87e413aa104714be4edaf077777a42a25ef41b3fe6952ba7e14744d565fe1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97721" :question/id #uuid "4c4fa733-f9a5-51a2-9ccc-317ee77679df" :question/math-academy-id "q-97721" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=2-3t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2-3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(4-12t+9{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(4t-6{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4-6+3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3t}{1} \\\\  & =2-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97721/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97721"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5908"] :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5548"] :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5549"] :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5550"] :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
